routes.what:
The owner, or an agent driving the existing browser-media-factory flow with approval per batch, prompts Gemini (Nano Banana 2/Pro) or ChatGPT Images for evenly lit, flat images:
- leaf sheets, single leaves and needle or scale sprays;
- flower heads: coneflower, rudbeckia, bergamot, Annabelle floret, panicle hydrangea panicle, Karl Foerster plume;
- bark, mulch and leaf-litter tiles.
Each image is produced twice, once on pure white and once on pure black, so a difference matte gives real alpha; a green background with a key is the fallback. The images are then matted, de-lit and colour-calibrated, and packed into the shared leaf-cluster, conifer-spray, petal/plume and bark atlases that PLAN.md already specifies. They replace or augment the SVG masks and CC0 detail tiles.

routes.species_coverage:
Plausibly usable only as opaque shells or hulls for dense or clipped forms:
- boxwood, yew and Smaragd (as a hull);
- the blue spruce silhouette at distance;
- Annabelle and panicle hydrangea heads as separate parts;
- possibly a hosta clump and a coneflower head.
Not usable at close range for any tree (sugar maple, red maple, white pine, serviceberry, redbud), any grass, the ostrich fern, or fine and medium-textured shrubs and perennials: spirea, ninebark, dogwood, lilac, catmint, salvia, bergamot, daylily, black-eyed Susan clump.

routes.what:
Photograph a potted specimen on a turntable against a backdrop: 8–16 azimuths at 1–2 elevations. Then:
- matte with BiRefNet;
- estimate depth with Depth Anything V2 Small for parallax and a shadow-casting proxy;
- in a runtime shader, pick or blend the nearest views.
Useful as LOD1/LOD2, or near-field at eye level. Precedent: ArchVision RPC's photographed basis views, which per the University of Kentucky paper have no interpolation between views.

routes.realism:
Photographic from near-horizontal cameras. Breaks down:
- from the Above camera;
- under DeckCraft's sun and HDRI, because lighting is baked (capture overcast to minimise this);
- when switching between views, which pops;
- at installed size, because nursery pots are smaller than installed plants.
